#show: main.with(
  title: "Too Much Of A Good Thing",
  desc: "",
  date: "2026-06-30T20:36:03-04:00",
  tags: ("ai",),
)

I've often thought it would be really cool if LLMs were great at understanding audio. Currently, frontier LLMs seem to do some kind of text-to-speech to pass your voice down to LLMs, and this gets rid of lots of interesting nuance. This means you can't use them for critiquing your music skills.

But I'm sure they'll solve this problem soon, and I'm a little worried what would happen if they did. On the one hand, everyone can get one-on-one musical coaching (maybe even for free?). On the other hand, how many people would go out of their way to pay for music lessons if they could just ask ChatGPT? Sure, some people will have a strong connection with their teacher, will be so good the LLMs can't really compete (in the near future) or similar. But a lot of people are just casual hobbyists. They might've been the people who eventually signed up for music lessons and will now just use this very convenient service.

This is a problem, because most people who do music full-time don't make enough money off of their performances. They rely very heavily on teaching for income, so if half as many people need a teacher... well....

This leads you to a rather counter-intuitive conclusion: democratizing music lessons might end up worsening the lives of musicians.

This isn't even that unprecedented of a thing to happen. Democratizing access to a decent question-answering service (ChatGPT) oblierated (in an incredibly short timescale) the rather more restrictive predecessor (i.e. wading through Stack Overflow toxicity).

I think the general point here is that democratizing something cheapens how valuable it is. Music lessons were valuable when you needed this person with years of accumulated experience to adjudicate you. StackOverflow was valuable when you needed the tricks of a SWE who'd been in the industry for 20 years. Now that these resources are becoming more plentiful, there is less marginal value in producing more.

You could take this to its natural conclusion and start worrying about if anyone will be able to make a living doing any skill, but that's not really my concern. I'm sure we'll figure out something for all of us to do. I'm just mourning the fact that maybe certain fulfilling lifestyles might now become far less inaccessible.